#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523} :activity/id #uuid "27341ce7-bad3-5506-8e90-5fea806b76fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125759 :tutorial/content "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n| **** | **$\\mathbb{R}^2$** | **$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Addition is Commutative:** |  |  |\n| A1 | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ | For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$ |\n| **Addition is Associative:** |  |  |\n| A2 | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ | For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$ |\n| **Existence of a Unique Zero Element:** |  |  |\n| A3 | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ | For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$ |\n| **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:** |  |  |\n| A4 | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ | For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$ |\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3184 :tutorial/title "Addition of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "f746dfa7-8c4b-5575-86b0-34ab4d9d09e6" :step/math-academy-id 17452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124297 :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369901}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"} :step/id #uuid "22604921-be1a-5e8f-bdd7-8ac9e59c63a0" :step/math-academy-id 17454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124400 :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371264}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "7b0d0289-9feb-5b9b-9cdf-8e8bea357afd" :step/math-academy-id 30785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125832 :tutorial/content "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n| **** | **$\\mathbb{R}^2$** | **$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:** |  |  |\n| A5 | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ | For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$ |\n| **Scalar Multiplication is Associative:** |  |  |\n| A6 | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$ |\n| **Distributive Laws:** |  |  |\n| A7 | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$ |\n| A8 | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ | For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4934 :tutorial/title "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" :step/math-academy-id 30789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125833 :tutorial/content "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nIn this context, we often refer to individual elements of [math]\\textrm{M}_2(\\mathbb R)[/math] as **vectors**. This helps us to draw parallels with vectors from [math]\\mathbb R^n.[/math]\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4935 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" :step/math-academy-id 30790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124432 :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329743}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" :step/math-academy-id 37506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125860 :tutorial/content "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$ \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." :tutorial/id #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6230 :tutorial/title "Sets That Are Not Closed"} :step/id #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" :step/math-academy-id 37838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125861 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6231 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space"} :step/id #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" :step/math-academy-id 37839}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124706}]}
